Page WebGL contexts share one host context.

Chromium caps WebGL contexts per worker at four, so giving every page context
a host context of its own capped pages at three and put the engine's context
at risk. Now all page contexts share one host WebGL 2 context (the engine is
built for WebGL 2 only, so WebGL 1 contexts already ran on WebGL 2 host
contexts). Each keeps a shadow of the state it sets and of the objects it
creates; when another context becomes current the host is moved from one
shadow to the next, setting only what differs. Stand-ins replace vertex array
0 and transform feedback 0 per context, errors are kept per context, and a
deleted program stays usable where it is current. A host context of its own
remains the fallback when the shared one cannot be had.

